Subject: [PATCH 054/154] feat: rebuild the settings writer, space cycling,
 crash recovery and the quit route
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

H4 finished tonight: all five chrome surfaces are back as HTML surfaces and a
settings edit now survives a relaunch, proven by reading the byte off disk
rather than by the app reporting success.

What is in here:

- Settings persist. SaveSettingsJSON reaches atrium_core_save_settings, the
  window posts through one sequenced runner, and a refusal reaches the user
  rather than only the log. The save has no lock, so a bare thread-pool post
  dropped the newer of two fast toggles.
- Space cycling works again. CycleSpace and SelectSpace give the two hotkey
  routes and the rail one path, and the new space's first row is materialised
  so live tabs follow the rail.
- The sidebar's active-row pill paints again. LiveStrip carries the active
  flag and --selfill mirrors the fill the Swift palette used to produce.
- Renderer crash recovery is wired. AtriumChromeContentsObserver attaches at
  CreateChromeContents, the one chokepoint all five surfaces pass through. It
  had never been constructed since the day it was written.
- Cmd+Q reaches the menu again. A surface with a live renderer was claiming
  the chord, because CommandDispatcher only calls postPerformKeyEquivalent
  when the view hierarchy declines and a live RenderWidgetHostViewCocoa never
  does. One shared decision now serves the palette, peek and the delegate.
- Three command decisions moved to the Rust core and their C++ bodies are
  gone. ABI 6 to 8.

Verified as a whole rather than per-slice: the bundle builds, 196 C++ tests
pass, and the scenario suite runs 43 with 31 clean.

Signed-off-by: János Mikó <693558+janosmiko@users.noreply.github.com>
